Introduction

Libraries serve as vital hubs of knowledge and information, but navigating their extensive collections can be daunting for visitors, especially newcomers. This abstract introduces an innovative solution – an Augmented Reality (AR) based wayfinder system designed to enhance the library user experience. Leveraging the power of AR technology, this system offers an intuitive and interactive means of guiding patrons within the library space.

By integrating AR features into a user-friendly mobile application, patrons can access real-time, visually enhanced navigation assistance. The system utilizes markers and sensors strategically placed throughout the library to provide accurate positioning data to the user’s device. Users can simply point their smartphones or tablets in different directions to view overlaid digital information, such as directional arrows, floor maps, and highlighted pathways, superimposed on the live camera feed.

This AR-based wayfinder system not only simplifies the process of locating specific books, sections, or amenities within the library but also enriches the overall user experience. It promotes independent exploration, encourages engagement with library resources, and fosters a seamless connection between patrons and the vast reservoir of knowledge housed within the library.

This abstract provides a glimpse into the potential of AR technology in transforming traditional libraries into interactive, digitally augmented spaces, enhancing accessibility, and ensuring that patrons make the most of their library visits.
Background Study

In the publishment by Mi Jeong et.all. [1] ,a hospital was selected as the context for a study on wayfinding in complex environments, and a specially designed instrument was created to comprehend the variables influencing the cognitive process of wayfinding. As discussed in work by Mutis et.all [2] the research explores Augmented Reality (AR) navigation, which requires proficiency in sensor technologies, computer vision, and geographic mapping. Same approach has been used by researchers in their respective works [4] [5] [9] . Articles highlight features like object detection, real-time tracking, and user interface in their discussions of the application of AR frameworks like ARKit and ARCore.

Using the Wikitude AR SDK and Unity software, a prototype augmented reality application for Geodesy faculty students is described in the work of Cibilic, et.all [3] ..Another paper published by Templin,et.all [7] addresses the difficulties in wayfinding in complex environments, especially in an academic setting (University of Technology, Malaysia). By utilising augmented reality technology, spatial planners and information designers hope to improve wayfinding aids and interactions. The article offers information on augmented reality (AR) technology, its hardware elements, and where it falls in relation to virtual reality (VR) and telepresence. A different study by Qiao et.all [10] examines how mobile augmented reality might improve interactions, focusing on striking a balance between virtual data and real-world perspectives to allay security worries.Papers on AR-based navigation systems are numerous. In the Paper by Gu et.all [11] presents a Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM)-based AR-based indoor navigation system that creates point clouds maps.

To increase the SINS/GPS integrated navigation system’s dependability, another paper by, Lyu et.all [12] suggests an information fault detection technique. Furthermore, a project of Sayapogu et.all [13] investigating the use of AR for indoor navigation in a large campus makes use of Unity, ARCore, and Navmesh. There is also discussion of intelligent and accurate AR-based navigation systems in the paper published by Wang, [14] with a focus on multifunctional AR navigation features and continuous positioning technology. Finally, paper of AR Navigation Application based on iOS Platform by Wang et.all [15] describes how to create an iOS platform augmented reality navigation application. This application offers real-time GPS-based location, 3D position estimation, and augmented reality scenes superimposed on real-world backgrounds. It consists of modules named Location, Digital Map, Approximate, Estimate, and AR. In conclusion, these efforts together address issues and investigate creative approaches for improved user experience while also helping to comprehend and apply AR technology in a variety of navigational scenarios.
System Design

The AR wayfinding system starts with a mobile application installed on the user’s smartphone or tablet. The application serves as the user interface through which they interact with the AR wayfinding features. It can be a standalone app or integrated into an existing library app. The mobile device’s sensors, such as GPS, accelerometer, gyroscope, and compass, provide essential input to the AR wayfinding system. These sensors help determine the user’s location, orientation, and movement, which are crucial for accurate positioning and alignment of the AR content. To enable precise indoor positioning, the AR wayfinding system employs location tracking technologies. These technologies help determine the user’s position within the library and track their movements as they navigate.

High-precision GPS modules and inertial sensors are examples of advanced sensor technologies that can be integrated to greatly improve location tracking accuracy and responsiveness in augmented reality navigation. Furthermore, the integration of computer vision algorithms can improve the system’s capacity to identify and comprehend its surroundings, allowing for more accurate localization even in intricate metropolitan settings. This is achieved by utilising real-time environmental data from onboard cameras. Moreover, future positions can be predicted by integrating machine learning algorithms for predictive analysis based on past user motions, which enhances and anticipates the AR navigation experience.

Azimuth, or the horizontal angle of a user’s direction with respect to a reference point, is often calculated in augmented reality navigation utilising sensor data from the device. This entails making use of the magnetometers and accelerometers that are integrated within the device. While the accelerometer assists in tilt correction, the magnetometer determines the device’s magnetic north direction. Algorithms like sensor fusion or quaternion rotations can compute the azimuth precisely by merging these sensor readings. The computed azimuth is then utilised to ensure that virtual items appropriately match with the user’s physical orientation, improving the precision of AR navigation by aligning AR overlays or annotations with the user’s real-world view.

Fig.1 gives the overall system architecture. Initially, the developer need to provide a QR code in the library which will help the users to install and use this system. Then the system request to access the current location of the user. After giving permission, we can search for our required book by using the book name. By manipulating user’s point of interest, the system will display the AR bot that will guide user to the required book in a better way. This is how the wayfinding is done in this system. The system will acquire data, including the user’s present position within the library, once it has gained access to their location. The next step is campus, camera and GPS calibration. It indicates that the sensors for navigation will turn on automatically to perform the wayfinding. The Point of Interest (POI) means the position of the required book. The encoding of POI involves finding the path by using the previously mentioned sensors and the location information of the user and that particular book. Also, the calculations for obtaining the shortest path are carried out. After finding the path, the AR bot will be displayed on the screen to guide the user to reach the book.

