The interface of the system contains three options such as search, about us and exit. It is pictured in Fig.2 . Selecting the search button will bring the user to a second interface in which there will be a search bar. User can enter their desired book’s name to attain the navigation bot which will guide the user to its position. After obtaining the book, user can exit by simply clicking on the exit button.

In the process of creating an augmented reality (AR) navigation system, the first step involves storing the GPS coordinates of the desired destination in a database. Simultaneously, the system obtains the GPS coordinates of the user’s mobile phone, establishing the current location. The next crucial step is to retrieve the stored GPS coordinates of the destination from the database. Using this destination information and the current GPS coordinates, the system calculates the direction, also known as the azimuth, in which the mobile device needs to be oriented for the user to face the desired destination. Once the azimuth is computed, the AR navigation system overlays Points of Interest (POI) onto the user’s real-world view, indicating the correct direction in which the mobile device should be pointed to reach the destination. These POI markers are rendered in the user’s camera feed, providing a visual guide to the desired location. This interactive and intuitive approach enhances user navigation, making it easier for them to find their way by following the markers aligned with the calculated azimuth.

There are a few important optimisations that can be made to improve the efficiency of an AR navigation system in Unity. First, use level-of-detail techniques to expedite rendering and reduce the amount of resource-intensive graphical effects used. Reduce needless rendering overhead by dynamically rendering only the content that the user can see by utilising Unity’s occlusion culling feature. Furthermore, optimise code by putting effective algorithms for processing sensor data into practise. This will guarantee fast and precise location updates without putting system resources at risk. To cut down on load times, use asset bundling and asynchronous loading for your assets. To reduce needless computations, think about putting in place a caching system for frequently accessed AR data. These optimisations aim to greatly enhance the overall performance and responsiveness of the system by concentrating on effective rendering, astute asset management, and efficient code.

