Challenges

There are a number of difficulties in creating an AR wayfinder for a library using datasets and libraries. First of all, because of elements like changing lighting and occlusions, it can be challenging to ensure accurate spatial mapping in a library setting. Integration of external datasets with the unique layout of the library may present calibration challenges. Maintaining the accuracy of the AR annotations is also a challenge in dynamic library spaces with furniture that can be moved around or temporary displays. The integration of an effective dataset management system presents challenges in terms of storage, retrieval, and real-time updates when handling the variety of library content and possibly large datasets.
Conclusion and Future Works

AR wayfinding in libraries offers a modern and efficient solution to the challenges of navigating books in large library spaces. It enhances the user experience, improves accessibility, and provides opportunities for interactive engagement with library resources. Users can simply follow the virtual markers or arrows displayed on their devices, eliminating confusion and reducing the time spent searching for items or areas within the library.

The scope of future study in AR wayfinding is broad, encompasssing various aspects of user experience, technology development, interaction design, and societal impact. Exploring these areas, researchers can contribute to the advancement of AR wayfinding technologies and shape their practical applications in a wide range of domains, including navigation, tourism.

In future the authors propose to develop the system using an Unreal Engine which would facilitate a more realistic environment. It takes a sophisticated method to create an AR navigation system with Unreal Engine, making use of its powerful visuals and real-time rendering features. This entails utilising AR frameworks such as ARKit or ARCore, incorporating AR functionality into Unreal Engine’s Blueprints architecture, and utilising its spatial computing capabilities for real-world mapping and scene interpretation. As a result, the navigating experience is clear and visually appealing.

